---
id: org.dxos.ui.react-ui-scene.3d
name: SceneEditor3D
version: 0.1.0
---

The 3D variant of the scene editor: what `SceneEditor` does when its dimension config is `config3d`. The core spec
states everything both dimensions share; this spec states only what 3D adds or does differently — the Babylon
viewport and its camera, gizmos, face selection, drag-to-create with height, solids, mesh import and export,
materials and the coloured axes.

The 3D viewport (`Viewport3D`) draws the open scene's 3D layouts with Babylon through `Scene3DEditor`, which picks
with a ray against the scene and the ground plane, runs every gesture in memory and reports its end to the editor as
one `LayoutIntent`. As a picture it is `Scene3DView`: the authored view alone, with no grid and no orbit. This package
also holds the 2D config and viewport adapter; the 2D variant spec states them, beside the 2D engine.

## Dependencies

```mdl
uses org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas: The shared scene editor this variant configures — its parts, store, commands, history, clipboard, lock, roles, panels, inspectors and timeline hold in 3D and are not restated.
```

```mdl
uses org.dxos.scene: The data the 3D viewport reads and writes — `Layout3D`, `Element3D`, `Style3D`, `Camera3D`, solids and the composite transform.
```

## Extensions

| Term        | URI                          |
| ----------- | ---------------------------- |
| `uses`      | `dxos.spec.core/uses`        |
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`      |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0` |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`      |
| `decision`  | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`  |

## Types

```mdl
type Tool3D
  desc: >
    What a left press does in the 3D viewport, mapped from the editor's tool: select, orbit (the hand), a gizmo's
    move, rotate or scale, extrude a face, link two elements, create an element of a kind, or place a model object.
  literals: 'select' | 'orbit' | 'move' | 'rotate' | 'scale' | 'extrude' | 'link' | { create: kind } | { place: object }
```

## Components

```mdl
component Viewport3D
  desc: >
    The 3D viewport the editor draws: `Scene3DEditor` over the open scene, or `Scene3DView` as a picture. The look
    is the display panel's when it is a 3D look, CAD otherwise.
```

```mdl
component Scene3DEditor
  desc: >
    Arranges a 3D layout: picking, gizmos, linking, creating and placing on the ground, face selection and extrusion,
    and drilling into a composite's parts with the rest of the scene faded. Locked, only select and orbit work.
  props:
    document, scene, editing, selection, tool: Tool3D, readonly, theme, grid, snap, axes, face, camera
  emits:
    onSelect, onIntent, onEdit, onContextMenu, onFaceChange, onCameraChange, onResize
```

```mdl
component SceneRenderer3D
  desc: >
    The Babylon renderer under both: draws the render list (each element of the layout and of every composite's parts
    inside its frame, keyed by its path), outlines the selection, draws its bounding boxes, a selected face, the ground
    grid, the axes and the gizmos, and picks.
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Camera
  req F-1.1: The camera orbits a target: a drag with the hand (Orbit) or the middle button orbits, Shift pans, and
    the wheel zooms; the right button opens the context menu, as in 2D.
  req F-1.2: A trackpad pinch zooms, a two-finger swipe orbits the way the fingers move (Shift pans) where 2D pans,
    and a mouse-wheel notch zooms.
  req F-1.3: Holding Cmd lends the left button to the camera whatever the tool: a drag orbits, and Cmd+Shift pans.
  req F-1.4: Fit frames the bounds of the open layout's elements from the same angle; actual size keeps the authored
    camera and looks at the content's centre; zoom moves the camera nearer or further, and the readout is how near it
    is to the authored view, nearer reading higher.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Selection, gizmos and faces
  req F-2.1: A click selects, Shift toggles and empty space clears; a selection is drawn in the theme's focus colour
    as a silhouette outline and a world-aligned bounding box per element ([org.dxos.scene#D-58]).
  req F-2.2: Move, Rotate and Scale put a gizmo on the one selected element; it moves the mesh in memory and its
    release is one intent in the open layout's coordinates. A scale sets the scale it ends at ([org.dxos.scene#D-48]).
  req F-2.3: Snapping moves a gizmo by the grid unit, turns it by 15 degrees and scales by a tenth, and places a new
    element on a grid point ([org.dxos.scene#D-64]).
  req F-2.4: Double-clicking a plain element selects the face it lands on, drawn as a translucent pane in the
    selection colour over that side of its bounds; the Properties section names it. Double-clicking a composite drills
    into its parts instead ([org.dxos.scene#D-86], [org.dxos.scene#D-87]).
  req F-2.5: Escape cancels a shape being sized, then clears a selected face, then drills out of a composite.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Creating
  req F-3.1: The create tools are cube, sphere, cylinder and cone, then Link, which binds two clicked elements.
  req F-3.2: A drag on empty ground sweeps a shape's base, both corners snapped to the grid; releasing fixes it, the
    pointer's rise then sets the height (not for a sphere), and a click fixes that and makes the shape; Escape
    cancels, and a plain click places the default size ([org.dxos.scene#D-88], [org.dxos.scene#D-89]).
  req F-3.3: A shape created on the ground rests on it, its centre half its height above the point
    ([org.dxos.scene#D-60]).
  req F-3.4: A paste lands one grid unit further along each ground axis per paste.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Solids
  req F-4.1: Join makes a union of the selection and Subtract a difference of two (the first is the base, the
    second the tool cut from it), each a new solid element selected after it is made ([org.dxos.scene#D-52]).
  req F-4.2: Extrude drags a face along its normal by the distance the pointer moves along it on screen; a selected
    face is the one extruded, wherever the drag starts.
  req F-4.3: A solid is recomputed with Manifold when drawn, the WASM module loaded on first use; a solid that cannot
    be built draws the operands that exist, each as itself.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Meshes
  req F-5.1: Import mesh reads an `.obj` or `.glb` file into a model object and arms the place tool with it, so a
    mesh is defined once and each placement stores no more triangles; it is offered in the Objects section, and a
    file that cannot be read is reported as the notice.
  req F-5.2: Export STL saves the selection as drawn, or every visible element when nothing is selected.
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Looks and materials
  req F-6.1: The looks are CAD (flat parts with edges), Presentation (lit materials), Clay (matte solids) and Clay
    Light (Clay held light), each a default style catalog: CAD offers Part, Highlight and Clearance, Presentation
    Metal, Glass and Glow, the two Clays Body, Accent and Ghost. Every look can be picked in a read-only scene and in a
    host that stores no look.
  req F-6.2: An element's `Style3D` draws as a material over its hue and tone: a solid, tinted or glass surface,
    edges, opacity, metalness, roughness and glow.
  req F-6.3: Clay follows the app's light or dark mode, and Clay Light is always light: its bodies and the ground
    plane under them are the 2D soft ground of that mode (`SOFT_PALETTE`), lit softly, each solid body casting a soft
    contact shadow at its foot; an element's hue draws only as its edges, or as a rim on a solid sphere.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Display
  req F-7.1: The ground grid is faint minor lines one unit apart with stronger major lines, fading out from the
    centre; the display panel offers the lines grid and no guides.
  req F-7.2: The axes are drawn in red, green and blue: x and z across the ground grid, lifted off it, and y rising
    from its centre.
```

```mdl
feat F-8: The 3D Properties form and extras
  req F-8.1: The Properties form shows centre, rotation and scale as vectors, then label, layer and style; with a
    face selected, a line above it names the face.
  req F-8.2: A 2D scene can be drawn in 3D on the ground plane, each shape lifted to the 3D kind that matches it, and
    a 3D layout flattened to its top-down view; the clipboard lifts and flattens the same way.
  req F-8.3: A group is a composite placed by an element, as in 2D, and the ring and grid arrangers place a
    composite's elements.
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: In 3D the axes are coloured red, green and blue.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-7.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: 3D groups become composites placed by elements, as in 2D; the ring and grid arrangers move onto composites, the 3D-only `members` and `arrange` element fields are removed, and the `.dx` reader and printer map the existing group text onto composites with no grammar change.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-8.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-3: The x axis is red, y green and z blue; x and z lie across the ground grid, lifted off it so its centre lines do not fight them, and y rises from its centre to the same height. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-7.2]
  why: The usual convention, with y up as Babylon's scene has it.
```

```mdl
decision D-4: A trackpad's two-finger swipe orbits the camera the way the fingers move, where it pans in 2D; Shift pans, a pinch zooms, and a mouse-wheel notch zooms. Fills in org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas#D-13.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-1.2]
  why: The wheel is classified as 2D classifies it, and orbit is 3D's counterpart of 2D's pan, so the same fingers do the same kind of move.
```

```mdl
decision D-5: The 3D rail is Select and Orbit, then Move, Rotate, Scale and Extrude, then cube, sphere, cylinder, cone and Link; the extra commands are Join, Subtract, Export STL and Import mesh, the last offered in the Objects section. Fills in org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas#D-25.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-3.1, F-4.1, F-5]
  why: These are the tools and commands 3D had, grouped by the shared rail's categories.
```

```mdl
decision D-6: The 3D looks are CAD and Presentation, each a default style catalog with three named styles (CAD: Part, Highlight, Clearance; Presentation: Metal, Glass, Glow). Fills in org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas#D-15.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.1]
  why: Themes are default catalogs, so each look offers styles a person can apply as well as its defaults.
```

```mdl
decision D-7: Escape steps back one thing at a time in 3D: a shape being sized, then a selected face, then a drilled composite. Fills in org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas#D-13.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-2.5]
  why: Each is a state the person entered last, so one key leaves them in reverse order, as 2D's Escape leaves an edit before a drill.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: Add a Clay look to 3D, in light and dark mode: matte solids in the ground colour under soft, wide light, with soft contact shadows on a ground plane of the same tone, and the element's hue as edge or accent. Light and dark follow the app.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-9: The 3D Clay look and the 2D Soft theme share the decision and, where possible, the implementation: Clay reads the same soft ground tones per mode as the 2D theme, so both have the same ground colour in each mode.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-10: Clay clears to the 2D soft ground of the app's mode and lays a ground plane in that tone at the grid's plane, or just under the lowest item when one reaches below it, lit by one light straight down that casts the items' shadows through a coarse, widely blurred map; the bodies are lit by a sky light and a weak key from the upper left, harder in dark mode. Fills in [D-8] and [D-9].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.3]
  why: A ground lit only by a light straight down matches the background exactly where nothing shades it, so the plane has no visible edge; a coarse blurred map gives a soft pool under each item rather than a sharp footprint; on the dark ground the bodies need more light for their tops to rise above it.
  rejected:
    - A ground always at y = 0, which hides any item that reaches below it.
    - A shadow-only material, which needs a Babylon package the renderer does not otherwise load.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: A clay body's hue draws as its edges, in the hue itself, on every element whatever its `border`; a sphere, which has no edges, carries it as a narrow Fresnel rim instead. Clay links are a darker line of the ground, and the Clay catalog offers Body, Accent and Ghost. Fills in [D-8].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3]
  why: Edge rendering on a sphere draws stray dots at its poles, and a rim on a flat face washes the whole face in the hue, so each shape takes the accent it can carry.
  rejected:
    - Tinting the whole body a little towards the hue, which breaks the single-tone ground.
```

```mdl
decision D-12: Clay's ground lies at the grid's plane, or just under the lowest solid body when one reaches below it, and only solid bodies (fully opaque, not a composite's frame) cast shadows on it, through a 1024 map blurred with a 32 kernel; the ground writes no depth, so anything reaching below it still shows. The lights and colours of [D-10] stand. Fills in [D-8] and [D-9].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-10
  affects: [F-6.3]
  why: A see-through shell round the bodies (the encapsulated exemplar's Team sphere) put the ground at its foot, far below the bodies inside it, so their shadows landed away from them; the wide blur then spread each shadow into a halo that read as a shadow of nothing.
  rejected:
    - Lowering the key light's slant, which does not move a shadow cast straight down onto a ground set too low.
```

```mdl
decision D-13: A clay body's hue draws as its edges, in the hue itself, on every element whatever its `border`; a solid sphere carries it as a narrow Fresnel rim instead (bias 0, power 0.2), and a see-through sphere or a composite's frame carries none. Clay links are a darker line of the ground, and both Clay catalogs offer Body, Accent and Ghost. Fills in [D-8].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  supersedes: D-11
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3]
  why: A rim round a large see-through shell tinted everything seen through it in the hue, which broke the single-tone ground; edge rendering on a sphere draws stray dots at its poles, so a solid sphere keeps the rim.
```

```mdl
decision D-14: Create a light-mode version of Clay alongside the one that follows the app.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-15: Clay Light (`clay-light`) is a 3D look of its own, drawn as Clay in the light mode whatever the app's; `themeMode3D` resolves a look's mode as a 2D theme's `mode` does. Fills in [D-14].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3]
  why: It mirrors 2D's Soft Light, a theme id with a fixed mode, so the look picker, the stored view state and the stories treat it as any other look.
  rejected:
    - A mode override beside the look, which the 3D view state has no field for and the picker no control for.
```

```mdl
decision D-16: The 3D look picker is the scene editor's shared display panel, so the 2D rule that every theme is always selectable, in read-only scenes and in hosts that store no choice, holds for the 3D looks unchanged. Clay has no inset tone: 3D styles carry no tone, and a body sunk into the ground would need cut geometry, so indentation stays a 2D style choice. 3D links draw no arrowheads, so 2D's rule for where a head ends has nothing to apply to. Fills in [D-9].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [F-6.1, F-6.3]
  why: Recording where 2D's choices stop keeps the two dimensions' parity explicit rather than implied.
  rejected:
    - A recessed Clay body for an inset tone, which needs boolean geometry the renderer only has for solids.
```

